# Snapshots are byte-exact evidence. Their sha256 values are pinned in
# fixtures/provenance.md and, for the conformance corpus, asserted against the
# upstream manifest by the test suite. Line-ending normalization would rewrite
# those bytes on checkout and silently invalidate every pin — so these paths are
# never normalized.
fixtures/** -text
refs/** -text

# Source and docs normalize to LF in the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf

# LAST MATCHING LINE WINS in this file, so a path rule placed above the four
# generic rules is overridden by them: `git check-attr text -- <path>` is how to
# check what is actually in force for a path, and today it reports `text: set`
# for `fixtures/**` and `refs/**` JSON and Markdown, which is not what the
# comment at the top of this file says. Left as it is here and recorded rather
# than changed, because changing it rewrites bytes the pins are taken over.
#
# An evidence package is byte-exact and more brittle than a fixture: its
# SHA256SUMS is SIGNED, so a line-ending rewrite on checkout would make every
# digest in it wrong on someone else's machine while leaving ours right — the
# worst shape a verification failure can take. This rule is last so it holds.
packages/** -text

# The registry carries reports, methodology documents and pinned inputs BYTE FOR
# BYTE, and publishes the sha256 of each one in registry/index.json. A
# line-ending rewrite on checkout would make every one of those digests wrong on
# someone else's machine while leaving ours right, which is the same failure
# `packages/**` is protected from above. This rule is last so it holds.
registry/** -text
